Here's how to implement global error handling using ASP.NET Core's built-in middleware:

**1. Register Problem Details services and exception handler in `Program.cs`:**

```csharp
var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args);

// Add Problem Details support
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails(options =>
{
    options.CustomizeProblemDetails = context =>
    {
        context.ProblemDetails.Instance = context.HttpContext.Request.Path;
    };
});

var app = builder.Build();

// Global exception handler middleware
app.UseExceptionHandler(exceptionApp =>
{
    exceptionApp.Run(async context =>
    {
        var exception = context.Features.Get<Microsoft.AspNetCore.Diagnostics.IExceptionHandlerFeature>()?.Error;

        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (StatusCodes.Status404NotFound, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException => (StatusCodes.Status400BadRequest, "Invalid request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (StatusCodes.Status409Conflict, "Conflict"),
            _ => (StatusCodes.Status500InternalServerError, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        context.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;

        var problemDetails = new Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == 500 ? null : exception?.Message,
            Instance = context.Request.Path
        };

        await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(problemDetails, contentType: "application/problem+json");
    });
});

app.UseStatusCodePages(); // Handles non-exception error status codes as Problem Details

// Your endpoints
app.MapGet("/items/{id}", (int id) =>
{
    if (id < 0) throw new ArgumentException("ID must be positive");
    if (id == 0) throw new KeyNotFoundException("Item not found");
    if (id == 99) throw new InvalidOperationException("Item is locked");
    return Results.Ok(new { Id = id, Name = "Example" });
});

app.Run();
```

**Key points:**

- `UseExceptionHandler` catches all unhandled exceptions globally — no per-endpoint try-catch needed
- 500 responses intentionally omit `exception.Message` to avoid leaking internals
- Non-500 responses include the exception message as `Detail` for client debugging
- `UseStatusCodePages()` ensures even manual `Results.NotFound()` returns Problem Details format
- The response `Content-Type` is set to `application/problem+json` per RFC 7807

**Alternative (ASP.NET 8+):** You can use `IExceptionHandler` for a cleaner DI-friendly approach:

```csharp
public class GlobalExceptionHandler : IExceptionHandler
{
    public async ValueTask<bool> TryHandleAsync(
        HttpContext context, Exception exception, CancellationToken ct)
    {
        var (statusCode, title) = exception switch
        {
            KeyNotFoundException => (404, "Resource not found"),
            ArgumentException => (400, "Invalid request"),
            InvalidOperationException => (409, "Conflict"),
            _ => (500, "An unexpected error occurred")
        };

        context.Response.StatusCode = statusCode;

        await context.Response.WriteAsJsonAsync(new ProblemDetails
        {
            Status = statusCode,
            Title = title,
            Detail = statusCode == 500 ? null : exception.Message,
            Instance = context.Request.Path
        }, cancellationToken: ct);

        return true;
    }
}
```

Register it:
```csharp
builder.Services.AddExceptionHandler<GlobalExceptionHandler>();
builder.Services.AddProblemDetails();
// ...
app.UseExceptionHandler();
```

The `IExceptionHandler` approach is preferred in .NET 8+ as it supports DI (logging, telemetry) and multiple handlers in a chain.
